WEBVTT

00:00.900 --> 00:04.950
Mr. Theisen is honing his
deadly three-point precision

00:04.950 --> 00:06.670
on the basketball court.

00:06.670 --> 00:10.190
For one of his shots,
the height of the ball

00:10.190 --> 00:18.150
in feet as a function of
horizontal distance, in feet,

00:18.150 --> 00:21.760
y of x-- so here y
is a function of x.

00:21.760 --> 00:24.930
So the height must
be y because that's

00:24.930 --> 00:27.460
the thing that is a
function of something else.

00:27.460 --> 00:29.930
So this right over
here is height.

00:29.930 --> 00:34.070
So our y-axis is going
to represent height.

00:34.070 --> 00:35.890
And it is a function of x.

00:35.890 --> 00:38.620
So x must represent
horizontal distance

00:38.620 --> 00:41.780
because height is a function
of horizontal distance.

00:41.780 --> 00:46.520
So this right over here
is horizontal distance.

00:51.900 --> 00:53.610
Now, it's plotted below.

00:53.610 --> 00:57.180
Mr Theisen is standing
at x equals 0.

00:57.180 --> 00:58.790
So he's standing
right over here.

00:58.790 --> 01:01.850
This is Mr. Theisen, as I
draw my best attempt to draw

01:01.850 --> 01:06.370
a little stick figure
version of Mr. Theisen.

01:06.370 --> 01:09.180
That's not even an acceptable
stick figure right over there.

01:09.180 --> 01:14.110
So this is Mr. Theisen, and
he's standing at x equals 0.

01:14.110 --> 01:16.990
And at x equals 0, he is
shooting a basketball.

01:20.550 --> 01:23.470
And you see from the
function right over here

01:23.470 --> 01:28.680
that where the graph
intersects the y-axis,

01:28.680 --> 01:32.340
that tells us that's essentially
the height of the ball

01:32.340 --> 01:35.814
when x is equal 0, where it's
where Mr. Theisen is standing.

01:35.814 --> 01:37.230
And if we look at
this, this looks

01:37.230 --> 01:41.540
like it's 2, 4, 6 feet high.

01:41.540 --> 01:43.690
So that's really the
initial position of the ball

01:43.690 --> 01:46.420
when Mr. Theisen is
about to let go of it.

01:46.420 --> 01:48.020
Then he lets go of
it, and the ball

01:48.020 --> 01:50.960
goes in this
parabolic trajectory.

01:50.960 --> 01:53.590
It increases, increases,
increasing, increases.

01:53.590 --> 01:57.920
It looks like it hits a maximum
point right around there,

01:57.920 --> 01:58.720
roughly.

01:58.720 --> 02:01.560
That looks like it's
at about 16 feet.

02:01.560 --> 02:03.420
And then it starts to go down.

02:03.420 --> 02:04.970
And right over
here-- and this looks

02:04.970 --> 02:06.910
like it's about,
let's see, 22, 24,

02:06.910 --> 02:10.160
26 feet out-- it looks
like it hit something.

02:10.160 --> 02:12.432
And considering that
something is 10 feet high,

02:12.432 --> 02:14.640
it's reasonable to assume
that the thing that it hits

02:14.640 --> 02:15.650
is the goal.

02:15.650 --> 02:18.940
And especially
because the question

02:18.940 --> 02:21.990
states that he has deadly
three-point precision,

02:21.990 --> 02:24.110
we can assume it's not
crazy that he actually

02:24.110 --> 02:24.822
makes the goal.

02:24.822 --> 02:26.530
And so that's where
it goes into the net.

02:26.530 --> 02:28.400
And then the net forces
the ball to go down

02:28.400 --> 02:32.480
at a much steeper trajectory.

02:32.480 --> 02:34.600
And this is exactly, of
course, 10 feet high,

02:34.600 --> 02:36.240
the height of the goal.

02:36.240 --> 02:38.817
Now let's see which of
these interpretations

02:38.817 --> 02:40.400
are consistent with
the interpretation

02:40.400 --> 02:42.060
that we just did.

02:42.060 --> 02:44.400
The ball is released
from Mr. Theisen's hand

02:44.400 --> 02:46.030
at a height of 6 feet.

02:46.030 --> 02:47.380
Well, that looks exactly right.

02:47.380 --> 02:50.571
When x is equal to 0,
the ball is 6 feet.

02:50.571 --> 02:52.070
And not only is
that right, but that

02:52.070 --> 02:55.290
is the significance of the
y-intercept of this function.

02:55.290 --> 02:58.050
The y-intercept
is the value of y,

02:58.050 --> 03:01.600
the height when x is equal to 0.

03:01.600 --> 03:05.140
So that is indeed
the significance

03:05.140 --> 03:06.360
of the y-intercept.

03:06.360 --> 03:07.890
Let's look at
these other things.

03:07.890 --> 03:11.550
Mr. Theisen is shooting the
basketball from 26 feet away.

03:11.550 --> 03:13.100
Well, that's right.

03:13.100 --> 03:14.380
He's at x equals 0.

03:14.380 --> 03:16.550
The goal is at 26 feet away.

03:16.550 --> 03:19.836
But that's not the significance
of the y-intercept.

03:19.836 --> 03:21.460
That would be the
significance of where

03:21.460 --> 03:25.210
we saw this little point here
where the ball dropped down

03:25.210 --> 03:27.040
at a steeper angle.

03:27.040 --> 03:29.570
The rim of the basketball
hoop is 10 feet high.

03:29.570 --> 03:31.289
Once again, that's true.

03:31.289 --> 03:32.080
You can look at it.

03:32.080 --> 03:33.413
You can see it right over there.

03:33.413 --> 03:36.100
But that's not the significance
of the y-intercept.

03:36.100 --> 03:38.350
The maximum height that the
ball reaches is 16 feet.

03:38.350 --> 03:41.470
Well, once again, that
is true, but that's

03:41.470 --> 03:43.830
the significance of this
maximum point on the curve.

03:43.830 --> 03:46.650
That's not the significance
of the y-intercept.

03:46.650 --> 03:49.480
So we'll go with
this first choice.
